<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Underground Railroad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1780s–1860s</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Underground Railroad was an organised network of secret routes and safe houses by which enslaved people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> escaped, chiefly to the free Northern states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. It took shape in the 1780s among abolitionist societies in the North and grew steadily until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a> of 1863. Primarily the work of free and enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>, aided by abolitionists, it used railway terms: escapees were &quot;passengers&quot; and those who aided them &quot;conductors&quot;. One estimate holds that about 100,000 people had escaped through it by 1850.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/underground-railroad.jpg" alt="Underground Railroad" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eastman Johnson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_A_Ride_for_Liberty_--_The_Fugitive_Slaves_-_Eastman_Johnson_-_overall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
